  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing SaaS Dashboard Design**\n\nI\'m currently focused on high-density SaaS dashboard design. I\'m leveraging my existing frontend design skills and integrating references, specifically focusing on how to maintain clarity and cleanliness within a dense interface.\n\n\n**Refining UI Component Design**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into UI text cleanup and audits across the dashboard, timeline, and card components. I\'ve started by scrutinizing `VttsControlPanel.tsx` and `App.tsx`, and I\'m methodically removing unnecessary descriptive text, tutorial blurbs, and helper paragraphs to enhance clarity. My focus is on retaining essential labels, telemetry numbers, and interactive controls, per the user\'s explicit request. I am removing the verbose pain weights text as instructed.\n\n\n**Streamlining UX Text**\n\nI\'m now methodically removing verbose descriptions from several components. Specifically, I\'m cleaning up text in `SortableStopCard.tsx` (e.g., removing `stop.notes`) and `LegTransitPanel.tsx`, replacing descriptive paragraphs with concise sub-segment badges or labels. I\'m focusing on retaining essential information while enhancing visual clarity and reducing cognitive load. The goal is a high-density, clean interface. I have also removed text in `AddStopModal.tsx`.\n\n\n**Optimizing Data Presentation**\n\nI\'m now focused on refining the presentation of itinerary data. Specifically, I\'m cleaning up component text to enhance clarity. In `ItineraryItem.tsx`, I\'m keeping only the city, country, IATA code, and region for hub entries, removing the verbose tagline. In `RouteOptimizerModal.tsx`, I\'m removing the verbose subtitle and retaining concise status indicators and numerical comparisons. In `VoucherDrawer.tsx`, I\'m eliminating the subtitle and verbose promo descriptions, keeping essential information like codes, titles, savings telemetry, and constraints. I am also working to fix the basemap, by removing watermarks.\n\n\n**Verifying Map Tile Integrity**\n\nI\'m now verifying map tile integrity to ensure there are no watermarks. I\'m checking `https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/positron` by running `curl` commands to confirm a 200 OK status and absence of watermarks. I\'m also cross-checking with OSM raster and Wikimedia/OpenFreeMap `liberty` tiles. The goal is to ensure a clean and professional map display.\n\n\n**Visualizing Route Geometries**\n\nI\'m now focusing on visually representing routes, including flights, trains, and buses, using mode-specific line colors. I\'m leveraging geospatial libraries like `@turf/great-circle` to render smooth, great-circle arcs for flights in sunset orange. The goal is to accurately represent route geometry, including waypoints for trains and buses, using ground coordinates. I\'m focusing on incorporating these into the basemap.\n\n\n**Implementing Route Visualizations**\n\nI am now implementing route visualizations for the itinerary, focusing on flights, trains, and buses. I\'m building on existing geospatial integrations, rendering flights as smooth arcs in sunset orange, with an airplane icon. Trains are now represented with Emerald/Teal polylines and intermediate station dots. Buses are drawn as Royal Purple polylines with stop dots. Transfer junctions are rendered as white-bordered circles colored by connecting mode. The goal is a highly readable visual representation of the entire route.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
