  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Worker Configuration**\n\nI\'ve been examining the configuration for the web worker in `maplibre-gl`. The `maplibre-gl-worker.mjs` file seems entirely self-contained, with no external imports, which is interesting. I\'m now looking into how `maplibre-gl` handles setting the worker URL, specifically lines 13684-13695 in `maplibre-gl-dev.mjs`, to understand the interaction.\n\n\n**Exploring Worker URL Options**\n\nI\'m now investigating setting the worker URL using `import maplibreWorkerUrl from \'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?url\';` with `maplibregl.setWorkerUrl(maplibreWorkerUrl);`. I\'ve confirmed that `?url` is typed as `string` in `vite/client`. I\'m also curious about the effects of using `optimizeDeps: { exclude: [\'maplibre-gl\'] }` in conjunction with this approach, to understand the build implications.\n\n\n**Verifying Vite Asset Handling**\n\nI\'ve confirmed how Vite handles the worker file. During development, it serves the file directly. In the build process, Vite copies the worker into the `dist/assets` directory and provides the same-origin URL to `maplibregl.setWorkerUrl`. I\'m now curious about potential advantages of serving the worker through `public` or via `?url` in Vite 8. Furthermore, I\'m checking if `react-map-gl/maplibre` shares the `maplibre-gl` instance.\n\n\n**Confirming Shared Instance**\n\nI\'ve determined that setting `maplibregl.setWorkerUrl()` at the module top level before the map mounts successfully updates the global `config.WORKER_URL` within `maplibre-gl`. I\'ve confirmed Vite doesn\'t split dependencies between the imports in `WorldMapCanvas.tsx` and `@vis.gl/react-maplibre`, resolving them to the same pre-bundled module. Furthermore, I\'ve verified that `@vis.gl/react-maplibre` uses `maplibre-gl` directly and can also accept an injected `mapLib` parameter.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_16749396385596338696"
      name: "replace_file_content"
