  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Fixing Color Assignments**\n\nI\'m focusing on ensuring each destination in the itinerary modal gets a permanently assigned color. My current thinking revolves around anchoring the color to the `stop.id` or initial assignment, guaranteeing consistency across interactions. No more color swaps!\n\n\n**Refining Visual Cues**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into visual selection states. The goal is to avoid color swaps, so I\'m thinking of rendering date range pills and end-caps permanently in their assigned stop color. A subtle outer ring in that stop\'s color will be considered for the active stop. I\'m also ensuring the top destination tabs and bottom legend dots remain fixed to their stop color, and I\'ll use borders, bold text, and backgrounds to indicate selection instead of color changes. To implement this I will be inspecting `dateUtils.ts` and `MultiDestDatePickerModal`.\n\n\n**Assigning Colors Permanently**\n\nI\'m revisiting the color assignment logic in `/shared/dateUtils.ts` to ensure permanent stop colors, and removing the dependency on `selectedStopId` to avoid color shifts on selection. I am planning to replace the old logic that started each stop on the first color and shifted down by one, with a strategy that permanently assigns by stop index, or ideally by `stop.id`, to stabilize color assignments. I\'m aiming for Stop 1 = `#FF812E`, Stop 2 = `#0D9488`, and so on, keeping the color assignments fixed in place.\n\n\n**Stabilizing Color Assignments**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a robust strategy for persistent color assignments. My goal is to prevent color swapping during reordering. I\'m leaning towards assigning colors based on a deterministic `stop.id` mapping or a sequence index fallback. This ensures that a stop\'s color remains constant regardless of its position in the itinerary, even if reordered. I\'m mapping each `stop.id` to a unique color, maintaining a stable visual identifier, no matter what.\n\n\n**Clarifying Color Mapping**\n\nI\'m solidifying the color assignment strategy now. I think assigning colors based on `stop.id` or initial sequence index is the way to go. The idea is to have consistent colors, like `#FF812E`, `#0D9488`, etc. assigned to each stop, and it seems this will prevent the jarring color swaps when clicking around in the modal. Considering edge cases where users drag stops around, and questioning whether `stop.id` or initial sequence index is preferred.\n\n\n**Finalizing Color Logic**\n\nI\'ve decided to anchor the color assignments purely on the index, ensuring deterministic and stable colors. Using a deterministic index-based approach like `STOP_BADGE_PALETTE[idx % STOP_BADGE_PALETTE.length]` is clean and predictable. It provides guaranteed color assignments: Stop 1 = `#FF812E`, Stop 2 = `#0D9488`, and so on. Now, I\'m auditing the `MultiDestDatePickerModal.tsx` file for hardcoded colors, ensuring every instance uses the assigned stop colors consistently.\n\n\n**Updating Visual Cues**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `activeColor` variable into the visual styling, to ensure active stop indicators consistently reflect the assigned stop color. For the header icon, I\'m thinking of applying `activeColor` for a cohesive look. I will update the top destination selector tabs to consistently reflect the corresponding stop color when selected, abandoning previous orange-tinted styling for a more accurate visual representation. I\'ll test it out and ensure these indicators consistently use the proper stop color.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Styling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on styling the selected tab with its corresponding stop color. I will be making the tab\'s border and background use the assigned stop color. For the calendar grid, I need to ensure draft selection and middle nights use `activeColor` instead of hardcoded values, by updating the existing color assignments. I\'ll make sure to replace hardcoded values with appropriate colors from my assignments.\n\n\n**Adjusting Day Highlighting**\n\nI\'m now fixing the date highlighting in the calendar grid. I discovered that active stop middle nights were being rendered incorrectly with hardcoded orange. I\'m updating the logic to correctly apply the assigned stop color to all dates. Active stop days will now have a slightly richer opacity of the stop color, but always use the stop color. For text, I\'m planning to use a black or darkened version of the assigned stop color.\n\n\n**Implementing Visual Indicators**\n\nI\'m now focusing on visually indicating the active stop\'s dates. I\'m adding a subtle visual indicator on the start and end dates with a ring effect (e.g., `ring-2 ring-offset-1`) or a top/bottom border in the stop\'s assigned color. The ring will appear around the active dates. This will improve clarity and visual distinction of the selected dates.\n\n\n**Refining Active Date Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual representation of active dates. I\'m focusing on clarifying the display of start/end dates and middle nights. A subtle ring effect (`ring-2 ring-offset-1`) or top/bottom border on start/end dates will be implemented. I will refine the CSS to ensure that active stop dates have a clean, highlighted appearance across the date range. I will check for 1-night stays, ensuring a single pill gets the ring effect.\n\n\n**Improving Date Highlighting**\n\nI\'m now implementing a new way to highlight active dates, and I am leaning towards using `boxShadow` over `outline` because `boxShadow` properly respects `border-radius`, while `outline` has inconsistent behavior across engines. I\'m focusing on ensuring visual consistency for active dates, especially when using `rounded-l-full`, `rounded-r-full`, or `rounded-full` classes. Also, I am going to be fixing the hardcoded orange values in active date logic and replace them with `activeColor`.\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_17372370901930909589"
      name: "replace_file_content"
      arguments_json: "{\"AllowMultiple\":false, \"Description\":\"Lock each stop color permanently by stop sequence index rather than swapping to Sunset Orange on selection\", \"EndLine\":174, \"Instruction\":\"Update getStopColorMap so every stop keeps a permanent color from STOP_BADGE_PALETTE without overriding the selected stop to #FF812E\", \"ReplacementContent\":\"/**\\n * Permanently assigns each stop a fixed color from STOP_BADGE_PALETTE\\n * based on its sequence index (Stop 1 = #FF812E, Stop 2 = #0D9488, Stop 3 = #7C3AED, Stop 4 = #D97706)\\n * so colors never swap when selecting different stops.\\n */\\nexport function getStopColorMap(stops: TripStop[]): Record<string, string> {\\n  const map: Record<string, string> = {};\\n  stops.forEach((stop, idx) => {\\n    map[stop.id] = STOP_BADGE_PALETTE[idx % STOP_BADGE_PALETTE.length];\\n  });\\n  return map;\\n}\", \"StartLine\":152, \"TargetContent\":\"/**\\n * Assigns Sunset Orange (#FF812E) to the currently selected stop,\\n * followed by Teal, Purple, Amber for remaining stops.\\n */\\nexport function getStopColorMap(\\n  stops: TripStop[],\\n  selectedStopId: string | null\\n): Record<string, string> {\\n  const map: Record<string, string> = {};\\n  const secondaryPalette = STOP_BADGE_PALETTE.slice(1);\\n  let secondaryIdx = 0;\\n\\n  for (const stop of stops) {\\n    if (stop.id === selectedStopId) {\\n      map[stop.id] = STOP_BADGE_PALETTE[0]; // #FF812E\\n    } else {\\n      map[stop.id] = secondaryPalette[secondaryIdx % secondaryPalette.length];\\n      secondaryIdx += 1;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return map;\\n}\", \"TargetFile\":\"/shared/dateUtils.ts\", \"toolAction\":\"Locking stop colors in dateUtils\", \"toolSummary\":\"Lock stop colors in dateUtils\"}"
