  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Date Logic**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on `MultiDestDatePickerModal.tsx` and `shared/dateUtils.ts`. My focus is now on tracing all possible scenarios, starting with bug #1: the preceding stop collision/overlap issue. I\'m deep diving into `updateStopDatesAndCascade` in `shared/dateUtils.ts` and its `export` declaration to understand the root cause.\n\n\n**Investigating Collision Scenarios**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the overlap scenario. The critical piece appears to be in how `updateStopDatesAndCascade` handles date modifications. Specifically, I\'m examining scenarios where the user\'s date selection results in `newArrivalDate` being earlier than a preceding stop\'s `departureDate`, which causes an unwanted collision. I am checking the logic inside of the function to see the behavior. I will look at the interaction when `newArrivalDate` is after the `oldStop.departureDate`, or when only `newDepartureDate` has been altered. I am also assessing how collisions are handled when `newArrivalDate` is earlier than the `prevStop.departureDate`.\n\n\n**Refining Date Cascade Logic**\n\nI am now focusing on the auto-shift implementation. My goal is to ensure that when a stop\'s date changes, all subsequent stops shift forward correctly to prevent overlaps and maintain any user-defined transit gaps. I\'m examining the scenarios where the initial dates are already inconsistent, or where shifting causes issues. Specifically, I am focusing on how `updateStopDatesAndCascade` handles the case where `nextStop.arrivalDate` is less than `oldStop.departureDate`. I am assessing the scenarios where transit gaps are negative after a change. I am focused on the logic for `idx > targetIdx` and ensuring we preserve positive gaps after date edits.\n\n\n**Validating Gap Maintenance**\n\nMy focus is now on confirming that the date cascade function correctly maintains transit gaps, even when stops\' dates shift or overlap before user modification. I\'m deep-diving into the iterative process and how it ensures a minimum gap of zero between subsequent stops, even if initial dates are inconsistent. I\'m zeroing in on the logic within the `for` loop and how `originalGap` is calculated and applied to calculate the `nextArrival` date, especially ensuring gaps are zero or positive. I\'ve noted how we enforce a clean approach to prevent overlaps.\n\n\n**Clarifying Stop Overlaps**\n\nI\'m now thinking about specific edge cases. I\'ve been considering how to prevent overlapping stops and the implications of directly responding to user input. The best approach is to disable date selection that is earlier than the previous stop\'s departure date, or, if we cannot disable (e.g. API constraints), cleanly adjust the preceding stop\'s departure date and stay nights to ensure `prevStop.departureDate === activeStop.arrivalDate`. For example, Stop 2 can start on the checkout day of Stop 1. If date selection is impossible to disable, I need to prevent the UI calendar from allowing invalid selection or collision.\n\n\n**Refining Overlap Prevention**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical adjustment. My plan is to disable date selection earlier than the previous stop\'s departure date within the UI, ensuring that users cannot inadvertently create overlaps. This will also involve clamping within `updateStopDatesAndCascade`, so that `newArrivalDate` will never be earlier than the `prevStop.departureDate`. My reasoning is that it helps the user to understand the constraints by disabling the earlier dates.\n\n\n**Confirming No Overlaps**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `prevStop.departureDate` in `updateStopDatesAndCascade` ensures that earlier stop data isn\'t overwritten. My focus is now on ensuring stops don\'t occupy the same night.  I see that edits to any stop trigger a cascade forward or backward, preserving gaps. If stop dates are moved forward, following stops will also be updated forward, and if stop dates are moved earlier, then the stop dates are moved earlier. No overlap!\n\n\n**Addressing Overlap Issue**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on an overlap scenario. I\'m reviewing how the date adjustments work, specifically the logic flow when a user modifies a stop\'s date and the subsequent cascading effect. I see the overlap problem occurs when dates are edited, and I\'m tracing the data flow to see why stop dates can be made earlier than the previous stop. I am checking the function `updateStopDatesAndCascade` to ensure that it adjusts, or clamps dates, to prevent the `idx < targetIdx` from allowing any overlaps. The UI may not properly reflect this because it doesn\'t adjust.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Representation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the visual representation of transition days in the calendar. I\'ve observed that same-day handoffs, where a stop departs and the next arrives on the same date, currently lack clear visual cues. I\'m considering options like split-pills or subtle indicators to enhance readability. I\'m prioritizing a clean, easily understandable presentation within the calendar grid, ensuring a seamless visual transition on these checkout/transit days.\n\n\n**Clarifying Stop Edges**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a visual clash when representing stop transitions in the calendar. Currently, the transition from one stop to the next, especially in same-day handoffs (check-out/check-in), is causing overlap in the calendar display. For example, the ring on the end date of Stop A bleeds onto the start date of Stop B. I\'m prioritizing solutions to visually clarify these transitions, particularly how `alloc` renders the calendar cells. I\'m focused on adjustments to the current visual treatment.\n\n\n**Visualizing Handoffs Clearly**\n\nI\'m now thinking about visual enhancements for calendar handoffs. I\'m focusing on same-day transitions, especially same-day handoffs, and exploring the best visual cues. I\'m prioritizing readability, considering gap insertion between date circles and investigating split-pill or dual-color indicator options for same-day handoffs to ensure a crisp, non-overlapping representation on the calendar. My focus is on rendering both the departure and arrival in an intuitive way.\n\n\n**Visualizing Handoffs Clearly**\n\nI\'ve been thinking through calendar visual enhancements, specifically for same-day handoffs. The goal is clarity. I\'m prioritizing the visual solution, with a focus on same-day handoffs to ensure the calendar representation is easily understood. I am investigating split-pill indicators for same-day handoffs and will render both the departure and arrival date in an intuitive way.\n\n\n**Visualizing Handoffs Clearly**\n\nI\'m now thinking through calendar visual enhancements, specifically for same-day handoffs. The goal is clarity. I\'m prioritizing the visual solution, with a focus on same-day handoffs to ensure the calendar representation is easily understood. I\'m favoring a split-pill indicator with a linear gradient. For example, a gradient of the previous stop\'s color to the next stop\'s color to represent a same-day handoff. I am looking at making the active stop cell ring and padding never collide with neighboring cells. I am trying a gradient approach that shows that stop A ends the day stop B begins.\n\n\n**Refining Split-Pill Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the split-pill visual implementation. I\'m experimenting with `rounded-full` on the pill to create the end cap and start cap for a gradient of the previous and next stop\'s color. My focus is on ensuring the appearance has a breathing gap between stops. I\'m thinking through adding `ml-[2px]` on `isStart` cells when the adjacent preceding cell is `isEndNight`.\n\n\n**Implementing Split-Pill Design**\n\nI\'m implementing a split-pill to clearly indicate same-day handoffs. The linear gradient approach with a gap, like `linear-gradient(135deg, ${prevColor} 48%, ${alloc.color} 52%)`, appears effective in visually communicating stop transitions without collision. I will ensure a consistent visual experience across various stop selections and calendar states by verifying the `activeStop.id` condition.\n\n\\n\\n**Addressing Scrollbar Issue**\n\nI\'m addressing the horizontal scrollbar issue in the tab navigation. The problem stems from the tabs exceeding the modal\'s content width due to the `max-w-md` setting and padding. My current focus is to refine the styling of the tabs, like the `overflow-x-auto` property, to properly handle this excess content. I\'m examining the layout of the tab container within `MultiDestDatePickerModal.tsx` to find a good fix.\n\n\n**Fixing Scrollbar Visibility**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the cause of the horizontal scrollbar in the tab navigation. The current `max-w-md` setting, coupled with padding, causes the tabs to overflow the modal. I am considering replacing the scrollbar with `flex-wrap gap-2` or compact pills via `px-2.5 py-1 text-xs`. I\'m evaluating increasing the modal width to `max-w-lg` to accommodate more destinations while maintaining a clean appearance without clipping. My focus is on ensuring all tabs fit cleanly without overflowing or scrolling.\n\n\n**Refining Modal Footer**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the date picker modal footer. I am preserving the legend and \"Done\" button on a single row using flexbox properties within the footer. I\'ve confirmed that the legend and button remain horizontally aligned, regardless of content length. I am making updates to ensure that the content is horizontally constrained.\n\nI am examining how date interaction is handled based on the active stop. I\'m checking the `handleDayClick(iso)` function in `MultiDestDatePickerModal.tsx`, focusing on preventing date selections that overlap preceding stops, and adjusting styling. I\'m looking at how `activeIndex` and `minAllowedIso` are set and used to disable and visually style invalid dates in the calendar, offering a visual cue.\n\n\n**Clarifying Inactive Dates**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the approach to inactive dates. My goal is to maintain the visual clarity of the stop\'s dates, even when they are unavailable for selection. I am planning on keeping the active stop\'s color highlights on the calendar. I will just set `cursor-not-allowed` and `aria-disabled=\"true\"` to prevent selection, and prevent any color washout on dates earlier than `minAllowedIso` to maintain visual clarity. I\'m focusing on user clarity when interacting. I am updating to keep the highlights and not the opacity.\n\n\n**Enforcing Date Constraints**\n\nI\'m now implementing logic to clamp `newArrivalDate` so stops can never overlap. The UI will prevent a user\'s date selection that causes earlier stops to overlap. I\'ve now applied a clamp to `arrivalDate` to be at least the same day as `prevStop.departureDate`. I\'m calculating and applying `stayNights` and ensuring dates are consistent. I am ensuring that the `updateStopDatesAndCascade` function prevents these collisions in all circumstances. I am validating that dates won\'t be earlier, and that gaps are correct, when the clamp is applied.\n\n\n**Analyzing Date Cascade Examples**\n\nI\'m now tracing example scenarios for the date cascade, especially when altering stop durations. I\'m focusing on the calculations within the loop. Specifically, I\'m examining how transit gaps are preserved or adjusted when a stop\'s arrival and/or departure date is modified. I\'m looking for zero overlaps.\n\n\n**Tracing Date Transition Cases**\n\nI\'m now tracing same-day handoff scenarios. I\'m focusing on the `dayAllocationMap` in `MultiDestDatePickerModal.tsx` and how colors are assigned. My aim is to ensure proper visual representation, especially when a stop\'s departure date is the same as the next stop\'s arrival. I am looking at how `handoffFromColor` and `alloc.color` are assigned to the calendar cells. I\'m specifically examining the color transition and split-pill styling, and plan to visualize departures and arrivals in a seamless way.\n\n\n**Applying Visual Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now implementing the split-pill and gap adjustments for calendar visuals. Specifically, for same-day handoffs, I\'m rendering `isEndNight` dates in the previous stop\'s color with `rounded-r-full`, while the `isStart` date of the next stop is rendered with the split-pill gradient, `rounded-l-full`, and a `ml-[2px]` margin for a crisp separation. I\'ve considered that, if the departureDate has no stop, then I will simply show the current stop\'s check out, for calendar clarity. I will apply these concepts to `shared/dateUtils.ts` and `MultiDestDatePickerModal.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_1475853170175486990"
      name: "replace_file_content"
      arguments_json: "{\"AllowMultiple\":false, \"Description\":\"Clamp arrival date against preceding stop and cascade all subsequent stops sequentially with zero overlap\", \"EndLine\":93, \"Instruction\":\"Update updateStopDatesAndCascade to clamp against preceding stop departureDate and sequentially recalculate all subsequent stops preserving non-negative transit gaps\", \"ReplacementContent\":\"/**\\n * Updates a single stop\'s arrival/departure/nights while clamping against the preceding\\n * stop\'s departureDate (preventing overlaps) and sequentially cascading all subsequent stops.\\n */\\nexport function updateStopDatesAndCascade(\\n  stops: TripStop[],\\n  targetStopId: string,\\n  newArrivalDate: string,\\n  newDepartureDate: string\\n): TripStop[] {\\n  const targetIdx = stops.findIndex((s) => s.id === targetStopId);\\n  if (targetIdx === -1) return stops;\\n\\n  const prevStop = targetIdx > 0 ? stops[targetIdx - 1] : null;\\n  const clampedArrival =\\n    prevStop && newArrivalDate < prevStop.departureDate\\n      ? prevStop.departureDate\\n      : newArrivalDate;\\n\\n  const stayNights = Math.max(1, diffDaysIso(clampedArrival, newDepartureDate));\\n  const resolvedDeparture = addDaysIso(clampedArrival, stayNights);\\n\\n  const result = stops.map((s) => ({ ...s }));\\n  result[targetIdx] = {\\n    ...result[targetIdx],\\n    arrivalDate: clampedArrival,\\n    departureDate: resolvedDeparture,\\n    stayNights,\\n  };\\n\\n  // Sequentially cascade all subsequent stops forward/backward so next.arrival >= prev.departure\\n  for (let i = targetIdx + 1; i < result.length; i++) {\\n    const prevOld = stops[i - 1];\\n    const curOld = stops[i];\\n    const originalGap = Math.max(0, diffDaysIso(prevOld.departureDate, curOld.arrivalDate));\\n    const prevNew = result[i - 1];\\n    const nextArrival = addDaysIso(prevNew.departureDate, originalGap);\\n    const nextNights = Math.max(1, curOld.stayNights);\\n\\n    result[i] = {\\n      ...curOld,\\n      arrivalDate: nextArrival,\\n      departureDate: addDaysIso(nextArrival, nextNights),\\n      stayNights: nextNights,\\n    };\\n  }\\n\\n  return result;\\n}\", \"StartLine\":58, \"TargetContent\":\"/**\\n * Updates a single stop\'s arrival/departure/nights and shifts all subsequent stops\\n * by the departure date delta so transit gaps are preserved and dates never collide.\\n */\\nexport function updateStopDatesAndCascade(\\n  stops: TripStop[],\\n  targetStopId: string,\\n  newArrivalDate: string,\\n  newDepartureDate: string\\n): TripStop[] {\\n  const targetIdx = stops.findIndex((s) => s.id === targetStopId);\\n  if (targetIdx === -1) return stops;\\n\\n  const oldStop = stops[targetIdx];\\n  const stayNights = Math.max(1, diffDaysIso(newArrivalDate, newDepartureDate));\\n  const resolvedDeparture = addDaysIso(newArrivalDate, stayNights);\\n  const departureDelta = diffDaysIso(oldStop.departureDate, resolvedDeparture);\\n\\n  return stops.map((stop, idx) => {\\n    if (idx < targetIdx) return stop;\\n    if (idx === targetIdx) {\\n      return {\\n        ...stop,\\n        arrivalDate: newArrivalDate,\\n        departureDate: resolvedDeparture,\\n        stayNights,\\n      };\\n    }\\n    return {\\n      ...stop,\\n      arrivalDate: addDaysIso(stop.arrivalDate, departureDelta),\\n      departureDate: addDaysIso(stop.departureDate, departureDelta),\\n    };\\n  });\\n}\", \"TargetFile\":\"/shared/dateUtils.ts\", \"toolAction\":\"Enforcing sequential date chaining\", \"toolSummary\":\"Enforce sequential date chaining\"}"
